---
title: Project page
layout: home
description: One project as a case study — the head, a carousel of screenshots, the numbers, the brief and what came of it, the project log as a stepper, the comments, and a rail of widgets beside all of it.
eyebrow: Collections · Project
source: src/components/repo-card.css · content.css · carousel.css · comments.css
---
<section class="im-section im-wrap" style="padding-top: 1.5rem; --im-section-gap: 1.5rem">
	<p class="im-eyebrow">Collections · Project · {{page.title}}</p>
	<p class="im-lede">{{page.description}}</p>
	<p class="doc-source"><code>{{page.source}}</code></p>
</section>

<div class="im-with-aside im-with-aside-wide im-wrap-wide">
	<article class="im-stack" style="--im-stack-gap: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem)">

		{{#with demo.projects.[0]}}
		<figure class="im-projectcover">
			<img src="{{image}}" alt="" loading="eager">
		</figure>

		<header class="im-projecthero">
			<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
				<ol class="im-breadcrumb im-breadcrumb-chevron">
					<li><a href="/">{{> "icons/house"}}<span>Home</span></a></li>
					<li><a href="/collections/project/">{{> "icons/folder"}}<span>Projects</span></a></li>
					<li><span aria-current="page">{{name}}</span></li>
				</ol>
			</nav>
			<h1 class="im-projecthero-title">{{name}}<span class="im-repo-status" data-state="{{state}}">{{state_label}}</span><span class="im-repo-visibility">{{visibility}}</span></h1>
			<p class="im-projecthero-text">{{text}}</p>
			<p class="im-repo-facts"><span>{{icon "clock"}}{{took}}</span><span>{{icon "calendar"}}{{since}}</span><span>{{icon "git-commit-vertical"}}{{commits}} commits</span></p>
			<p class="im-repo-topics">{{#foreach topics}}<a class="im-topic" href="#">{{this}}</a>{{/foreach}}</p>
			<div class="im-projecthero-actions">
				<a class="im-btn im-btn-primary" href="#">{{> "icons/external-link"}}Live demo</a>
				<a class="im-btn" href="#">{{> "icons/brand-github"}}Source</a>
				<a class="im-btn" href="#">{{> "icons/book-open"}}Docs</a>
				<a class="im-star im-star-sm" href="#">{{> "icons/brand-github"}}<span>Star</span><b>{{stars}}</b></a>
			</div>
		</header>
		{{/with}}

		{{!-- The screenshots. A carousel wrapped in data-im-lightbox, so every
		      shot opens on the plain sheet at full size — a case study is the
		      one place a reader genuinely wants to look closely. --}}
		<section class="im-section" style="--im-section-gap: .75rem">
			<div data-im-lightbox>
				{{#> "components/carousel" label="Screenshots" dots=true style="--im-slides: 1.05; --im-slides-sm: 1.6; --im-slides-md: 2.1"}}
					{{#foreach demo.six}}<a class="im-zoomable" href="{{feature_image}}" data-caption="{{title}}"><img src="{{feature_image}}" alt="{{title}}" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 10; width: 100%; object-fit: cover"></a>{{/foreach}}
				{{/"components/carousel"}}
			</div>
		</section>

		<div class="im-projectstats">
			<span><strong>Design system</strong>Role</span>
			<span><strong>2026</strong>Year</span>
			<span><strong>Tailwind 4</strong>Built on</span>
			<a href="#"><strong>1,204</strong>Stars</a>
			<span><strong>MIT</strong>Licence</span>
		</div>

		<section class="im-content im-content-flush im-content-lede" data-im-zoom style="--im-content-main: 100%">
			<p>A design system a Ghost theme can be built on and sold with — tokens, a page shell, forty components and ready-made sections, written from a blank file so that nothing in it belongs to anybody else.</p>

			<h2>The brief</h2>
			<p>The site ran on a paid theme whose licence forbids redistribution and modification for resale. Anything built on it could never be sold. The brief was therefore not "make a nicer theme": it was <strong>own every line</strong>, and be able to prove it.</p>

			<h2>What I did</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Split the tokens in two.</strong> Primitives have no meaning; semantic tokens say what a value is <em>for</em>. A component names the second kind only, which is what made dark mode a change to one file instead of forty.</li>
				<li><strong>Wrote one interaction and deleted the rest.</strong> Everything fills. A border sharpening, a picture growing and a shadow lifting were three ideas competing for the same moment.</li>
				<li><strong>Made the docs the product.</strong> Every example on the site renders the real partial, so a page breaks if a component does.</li>
			</ul>

			<figure class="im-shot kg-card kg-width-wide">
				<img src="{{demo.six.[2].feature_image}}" alt="" loading="lazy">
				<figcaption><b>The token layer</b> One file per kind, and a bridge that hands them to Tailwind.</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<h2>How it works</h2>
			<p>Everything is a cascade layer, in one order, low to high:</p>
			<pre><code class="language-css">@layer theme, tokens, base, layout, components, effects, utilities;</code></pre>
			<p>A component can never accidentally beat a utility, and a utility can never accidentally beat a state — which is the whole reason a design system stops being a pile of overrides in its second year.</p>

			<figure class="im-case-quote kg-card kg-width-wide">
				<p>The tokens carried our rebrand in an afternoon. I had budgeted a fortnight and a fight.</p>
				<figcaption>{{> "components/avatar" size="sm" name="Mara Lindqvist"}}Mara Lindqvist · Design lead, Nordvik</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<h2>What happened</h2>
			<p>Three sites run on it, the theme it was written for is in the shop, and the thing I actually wanted — being able to answer "where did this come from?" about every file — is a two-line audit that comes back empty.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="im-section" style="--im-section-gap: 1rem">
			<h2 class="im-caption" data-index="tools">Built with</h2>
			<div class="im-toolgrid">
				{{#with demo.projects.[0]}}{{#each stack}}
				<span class="im-tool">{{icon this.icon}}<b>{{this.name}}</b><small>{{this.why}}</small></span>
				{{/each}}{{/with}}
				<span class="im-tool">{{icon "terminal"}}<b>esbuild</b><small>One bundle, no config</small></span>
				<span class="im-tool">{{icon "rocket"}}<b>GitHub Pages</b><small>Deploy on push</small></span>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="im-content im-content-flush">
			<h2>What I learned</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>A second skin is a second product.</strong> The style scope doubled the work and halved the testing, and it existed because it was interesting rather than because anybody asked. Deleting it was the single best day of the project.</li>
				<li><strong>Documentation that renders the real component cannot rot.</strong> Every example on the docs site uses the shipping partial, so a broken component breaks its own page. Screenshots in a README would have been wrong within a month.</li>
				<li><strong>Write the "why" comment while the decision is fresh.</strong> Half the value of this codebase is a hundred comments that say what breaks if a line changes. None of them could have been written six months later.</li>
				<li><strong>Constraints from a licence are still constraints.</strong> "Own every line" sounded like a legal detail and turned out to be the whole architecture.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Would I do it again</h2>
			<p>Yes, and in about half the time — nine months was four months of building and five of deciding what not to build. If you are starting one: pick the twenty components you actually use, write those properly, and add the twenty-first only when a real page needs it. Everything I regret is something I built for a page that never existed.</p>
		</section>

		{{!-- The log: the timeline component, tuned. A project's history is a
		      list of dated things in order, which is what a timeline is. --}}
		<section class="im-section" style="--im-section-gap: 1.25rem">
			<h2 class="im-caption" data-index="log" id="project-log">Project log</h2>
			<ol class="im-timeline im-timeline-numbered im-log">
				{{#each demo.log}}
				<li class="im-timeline-item" data-state="{{state}}">
					<span class="im-timeline-marker"></span>
					<time class="im-timeline-time">{{when}}</time>
					<h3 class="im-timeline-title">{{title}}</h3>
					<p class="im-timeline-text">{{text}}</p>
					{{#if shot}}<figure class="im-log-shot"><img src="{{shot}}" alt="" loading="lazy"><figcaption>{{title}}</figcaption></figure>{{/if}}
					{{#if tag}}<p class="im-log-meta"><span class="im-badge im-badge-outline">{{tag}}</span></p>{{/if}}
				</li>
				{{/each}}
			</ol>
		</section>

		{{> "components/comments" thread=demo.comments count="41"}}
	</article>

	<aside>
		<div class="im-sticky-rail im-sticky-rail-full">
			<div class="im-widgets">
				{{#with demo.author}}{{> "components/author-widget"}}{{/with}}
			<section class="im-widget" aria-labelledby="pw-at">
				<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="pw-at">At a glance</h2>
				<div class="im-widget-stats">
					<div><strong>1.2k</strong><span>Stars</span></div>
					<div><strong>84</strong><span>Forks</span></div>
					<div><strong>9 mo</strong><span>Built in</span></div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="im-widget" aria-labelledby="pw-back">
				<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="pw-back">This project</h2>
				<p class="im-widget-heading">im-design-system</p>
				<p class="im-cluster" style="--im-cluster-gap: .375rem">
					<a class="im-btn im-btn-primary im-btn-sm" href="#">{{icon "external-link"}}Demo</a>
					<a class="im-btn im-btn-sm" href="#">{{icon "brand-github"}}Code</a>
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="im-widget im-rail-grow" aria-labelledby="pw-log">
				<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="pw-log">The log <a href="/collections/project/log/">All</a></h2>
				<ol class="im-timeline im-timeline-compact im-widget-series">
					{{#foreach demo.log}}
					<li class="im-timeline-item" data-state="{{#if state}}{{state}}{{else}}done{{/if}}">
						<span class="im-timeline-marker">{{@number}}</span>
						<h3 class="im-timeline-title"><a href="#project-log">{{title}}</a></h3>
					</li>
					{{/foreach}}
				</ol>
			</section>

			<section class="im-widget im-widget-hire" aria-labelledby="pw-hire">
				<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="pw-hire">Want one of these</h2>
				<p class="im-widget-hire-state" data-state="available">Two days a week, from November</p>
				<a class="im-btn im-btn-primary im-btn-sm" href="/pages/contact/">{{icon "mail"}}Start a conversation</a>
			</section>
		</div>
	</aside>
</div>

<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>A repo card, opened out into a case study</h2>
		<p>The head is the <a href="/collections/project/card/">card</a> given room: the name becomes the page's title, the one line becomes a paragraph, the topics stay exactly as they were, and the numbers move from a meta line into <code>im-projectstats</code>. Everything after it is a component that already existed — a carousel, the post body's canvas, the timeline, the comment thread — which is the test a page like this has to pass. If a project page needs eight new classes, the system did not have enough in it.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Built from</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>The head</td><td><code>im-projecthero</code> + <a href="/navigation/breadcrumb/">breadcrumb</a>, <code>im-topic</code> chips, <code>im-projecthero-actions</code>. <strong>Three buttons at most</strong>: a row of nine is a row nobody presses.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Screenshots</td><td>The <a href="/components/carousel/">carousel</a> wrapped in <code>data-im-lightbox</code>, slides as <code>im-zoomable</code>. Every shot opens on the plain sheet at full size — a case study is the one place a reader genuinely wants to look closely.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The facts</td><td><code>im-projectstats</code> — role, year, stack, stars, licence. Cells sharing a hairline; any of them may be a link.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The case</td><td><code>im-content</code>, the same canvas a <a href="/collections/post/article/">post</a> uses, with <code>im-shot</code> for a wide screenshot and <code>im-case-quote</code> for the one line somebody said.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The log</td><td><code>im-timeline im-timeline-numbered im-log</code> — the <a href="/components/timeline/">timeline</a>, tuned. A log is a list of dated things in order, which is exactly what a timeline is; a second component that drew the same line would be a second component to keep in step.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Comments</td><td><code>im-comments</code>, the same thread as a post's.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>

		<h3>The log is a stepper</h3>
		<p>Numbered marks down a line, and each entry carries <code>data-state</code>: <code>done</code> fills the mark in ink and draws a solid line to the next one, <code>current</code> puts it in the accent with a ring, <code>upcoming</code> fades the entry and dashes the line. A reader can see at a glance where the project got to and whether it is still moving.</p>
		{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
<ol class="im-timeline im-timeline-numbered im-log">
	{{#foreach log}}
	<li class="im-timeline-item" data-state="{{state}}">
		<span class="im-timeline-marker"></span>
		<time class="im-timeline-time">{{when}}</time>
		<h3 class="im-timeline-title">{{title}}</h3>
		<p class="im-timeline-text">{{text}}</p>
		{{#if shot}}<figure class="im-log-shot"><img src="{{shot}}" alt=""></figure>{{/if}}
	</li>
	{{/foreach}}
</ol>
		{{{{/code}}}}
		<p>The writing <em>about</em> a project is a different thing again, and it belongs in the <a href="/collections/post/">post collection</a> as ordinary posts — the <a href="/collections/project/">project collection</a> prints the latest few of them under the grid. A log entry is "this changed"; a post is "here is why".</p>

		<h3>The rail keeps one distance</h3>
		<p>The widgets are an <code>im-widgets</code> column in an <code>im-sticky-rail</code>, which is the same pair the <a href="/collections/post/article/#both-rails-keep-the-same-distance">post page</a> uses. <strong>One</strong> variable does it: <code>--im-rail-inset</code> is the distance under the top bar <em>and</em> the gap the rail keeps above the bottom of the window, and <code>im-widgets</code> has one gap between every block. Nothing on a project page is spaced by hand.</p>

		<h2>Details that matter</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>The widest column wins for code.</strong> <code>--im-content-main: 100%</code> — a README or a case study is mostly commands and lists, and nobody reads an install line as prose.</li>
			<li><strong>Screenshots get captions that say what is being looked at.</strong> "The token layer", not "Screenshot 3".</li>
			<li><strong>One quote, in the middle.</strong> Where it answers the paragraph above it — not a wall of testimonials at the bottom that nobody reaches.</li>
			<li><strong>Comments on a project are worth more than on a post.</strong> They are usually the bug report.</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</section>
